<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="Account Read — Load (stepped capacity verification)" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Load test: verify the capacity BOUNDARY discovered by the account-read
stress test holds under sustained load — not just a 30s rolling window.

╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║ BOUNDARY (from realistic-pacing stress run): 650 VUs                 ║
║                                                                      ║
║   BOUNDARY      = 650 VUs   (last fully clean step in the timer-     ║
║                              enabled stress run; errors first        ║
║                              appeared above 650)                     ║
║   70% BOUNDARY  = 455 VUs   (warm-up / recovery level)               ║
║                                                                      ║
║ At this scale the conventional ±1 VU steps (B-1, B+1) are            ║
║ statistically indistinguishable, so the plan uses ±~5% brackets      ║
║ instead — equivalent in relative terms to the ±1 VU spacing in the   ║
║ withdraw load plan (where ±1 ≈ 8% of B=12):                          ║
║                                                                      ║
║   warm-up         = 455 VUs   ( 70% — same as recovery)              ║
║   just-under      = 620 VUs   (~95% — confirms approach is stable)   ║
║   at boundary     = 650 VUs   (100% — the 10-min hold)               ║
║   just-over       = 680 VUs   (~105% — should start to crack)        ║
║   recovery        = 455 VUs   ( 70%)                                 ║
║                                                                      ║
║ The earlier 3-5 VU "boundary" measured without the Gaussian timer    ║
║ was a load-generator artefact (Windows ephemeral-port exhaustion at  ║
║ high connection churn for fast reads). With the timer applied in     ║
║ the new stress run, the true realistic-pacing server capacity is     ║
║ two orders of magnitude higher.                                      ║
║                                                                      ║
║ ⚠ Load-generator memory: 680 JVM threads × ~1 MB stack each ≈        ║
║ ~700 MB heap just for thread stacks at peak. Default JMeter heap     ║
║ (1 GB) should hold but consider setting HEAP="-Xms1g -Xmx2g"         ║
║ before running to be safe.                                           ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

This is a HYBRID load test — a stepped capacity scan with recovery — not a
single-level steady-state load test. Each step holds long enough to expose
slow degradation (connection accumulation, pool starvation, memory creep).

Virtual users are spread across MANY distinct accounts (tests/stress/data/accounts.csv,
1000 rows) via CSVDataSet round-robin (shareMode.all + recycle), so single-row
read contention is NOT a factor. We're measuring the system, not single-account
behaviour.

This exercises the READ path: plain SELECT, no FOR UPDATE, no $transaction,
no transactions-table write. Postgres query cache and Prisma connection pool
are the most likely saturation points.

Shape (Ultimate Thread Group, 28 min total):

  ┌──────────────┬──────┬───────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Window       │ VUs  │ Hold  │ Maps to spec                    │
  ├──────────────┼──────┼───────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 0–5 min      │  455 │  5 m  │ 70% of boundary    (warm-up)    │
  │ 5–10 min     │  620 │  5 m  │ ~95% of boundary   (just under) │
  │ 10–20 min    │  650 │ 10 m  │ boundary           (at boundary)│
  │ 20–25 min    │  680 │  5 m  │ ~105% of boundary  (just over)  │
  │ 25–28 min    │  455 │  3 m  │ 70% of boundary    (recovery)   │
  └──────────────┴──────┴───────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

Pass criteria per phase (same gates as stress):
  - p95 latency &lt; 5000ms
  - error rate &lt; 1%

Response Assertion: assume_success=true — for the read endpoint there is
NO 422 path (a read either succeeds with 200 or fails — see below). The
assertion still requires exactly 200, so any 403 / 404 / 5xx / timeout
counts as an error. assume_success=true means only assertion failures and
client/transport errors (BindException, connect timeout) count toward the
1% budget; a missed assertion still counts. This is consistent with the
withdraw / deposit load plans.

Think time: a Gaussian Random Timer is attached to the sampler (mean
3000ms, deviation 1000ms — total delay ≈ 3000ms + |N(0, 1000)| per
iteration). This models realistic user pacing: a customer opens the app,
checks their balance, then continues. Without it each VU would loop as
fast as the server replies (potentially 100+ req/s for a fast read),
which is a saturation pattern, not a load pattern.

Consequence for VU counts: the boundary VU number above was measured
*without* think time. With the timer, each VU now contributes ~0.3
req/s. So this load run no longer reproduces the stress-run throughput
at the same VU count — it measures whether the system stays healthy
under sustained *realistic* concurrency, not under saturating load.

⚠ Windows load-generator caveat: at the 680-VU just-over phase the
aggregate request rate is ~204 req/s (680 × 0.3 req/s) — above the
~68 conn/s ephemeral-port ceiling. HTTP keepalive (enabled on the
sampler) means JMeter reuses connections rather than opening one per
request, so the actual TCP-connection-open rate stays well under that;
exact figure depends on the server's keepalive behaviour. If
BindException starts appearing in the JTL during the just-over phase,
that's a load-generator finding, not a server finding. Apply the
netsh / TcpTimedWaitDelay tweak from tests/stress/RESULTS.md §6 / E-1
to confirm.

Run headless from PROJECT ROOT:
  jmeter -n -t tests/stress/jmeter/load/account-read.load.jmx \
         -l tests/stress/reports/account-read-load.jtl \
         -e -o tests/stress/reports/account-read-load-html

Or via the wrapper script:
  npm run test:load:account-read

Prereqs:
  1. npx tsx tests/stress/scripts/seed.ts
  2. Server running on localhost:3000 (npm run build &amp;&amp; npm start)
  3. JMeter Plugin: jpgc-casutg (Custom Thread Groups)</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables" enabled="true">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
          <elementProp name="BASE_URL" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">BASE_URL</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(host,localhost)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="PORT" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">PORT</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(port,3000)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="DATA_DIR" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">DATA_DIR</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(dataDir,tests/stress/data)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
      </elementProp>
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.user_define_classpath"></stringProp>
    </TestPlan>
    <hashTree>
      <ConfigTestElement guiclass="HttpDefaultsGui" testclass="ConfigTestElement" testname="HTTP Request Defaults">
        <intProp name="HTTPSampler.connect_timeout">5000</intProp>
        <intProp name="HTTPSampler.response_timeout">15000</intProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.domain">${BASE_URL}</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.port">${PORT}</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.protocol">http</stringProp>
        <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="HTTPArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables">
          <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments"/>
        </elementProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.implementation"></stringProp>
      </ConfigTestElement>
      <hashTree/>
      <HeaderManager guiclass="HeaderPanel" testclass="HeaderManager" testname="HTTP Header Manager">
        <collectionProp name="HeaderManager.headers">
          <elementProp name="" elementType="Header">
            <stringProp name="Header.name">Content-Type</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Header.value">application/json</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="" elementType="Header">
            <stringProp name="Header.name">Authorization</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Header.value">Bearer ${token}</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
      </HeaderManager>
      <hashTree/>
      <CSVDataSet guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="CSVDataSet" testname="accounts.csv">
        <stringProp name="filename">${DATA_DIR}/accounts.csv</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="fileEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="variableNames">accountId,userId,token</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="delimiter">,</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="quotedData">false</boolProp>
        <boolProp name="recycle">true</boolProp>
        <boolProp name="stopThread">false</boolProp>
        <stringProp name="shareMode">shareMode.all</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="ignoreFirstLine">true</boolProp>
      </CSVDataSet>
      <hashTree/>
      <kg.apc.jmeter.threads.UltimateThreadGroup guiclass="kg.apc.jmeter.threads.UltimateThreadGroupGui" testclass="kg.apc.jmeter.threads.UltimateThreadGroup" testname="Ultimate Thread Group — 455 → 620 → 650 → 680 → 455 (28 min)">
        <collectionProp name="ultimatethreadgroupdata">
          <collectionProp name="row-baseline-455">
            <stringProp name="utg-r1-threads">455</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r1-delay">0</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r1-startup">10</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r1-hold">1670</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r1-shutdown">10</stringProp>
          </collectionProp>
          <collectionProp name="row-step-620">
            <stringProp name="utg-r2-threads">165</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r2-delay">300</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r2-startup">5</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r2-hold">295</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r2-shutdown">0</stringProp>
          </collectionProp>
          <collectionProp name="row-step-650">
            <stringProp name="utg-r3-threads">195</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r3-delay">600</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r3-startup">1</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r3-hold">599</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r3-shutdown">0</stringProp>
          </collectionProp>
          <collectionProp name="row-step-680">
            <stringProp name="utg-r4-threads">225</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r4-delay">1200</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r4-startup">1</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r4-hold">299</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r4-shutdown">0</stringProp>
          </collectionProp>
        </collectionProp>
        <elementProp name="ThreadGroup.main_controller" elementType="LoopController" guiclass="LoopControlPanel" testclass="LoopController" testname="Loop Controller">
          <intProp name="LoopController.loops">-1</intProp>
          <boolProp name="LoopController.continue_forever">false</boolProp>
        </elementProp>
        <stringProp name="ThreadGroup.on_sample_error">continue</stringProp>
      </kg.apc.jmeter.threads.UltimateThreadGroup>
      <hashTree>
        <HTTPSamplerProxy guiclass="HttpTestSampleGui" testclass="HTTPSamplerProxy" testname="GET /api/v1/accounts/${accountId}">
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.path">/api/v1/accounts/${accountId}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.method">GET</stringProp>
